say, put this in backticks.

00:37:22.710 --> 00:37:25.240
That will make it a
code font, like so.

00:37:25.240 --> 00:37:33.850
We'll say, "The v1.0 build used
the first version of the offer

00:37:33.850 --> 00:37:40.030
agent SDK," because we're going
to explain that the offers were

00:37:40.030 --> 00:37:43.330
pretty bad up until the
second version came out.

00:37:43.330 --> 00:37:46.510
Just coincidentally,
it picked up.

00:37:46.510 --> 00:37:50.410
So we'll say the offer
system was broke initially.

00:37:50.410 --> 00:37:51.173
So we got that.

00:37:51.173 --> 00:37:52.840
Put a comma on the
outside, because this

00:37:52.840 --> 00:37:54.910
is the Markdown part.

00:37:54.910 --> 00:38:01.510
Locations, and this will
be cells_column_spanners(),

00:38:01.510 --> 00:38:06.730
and this is going to be spanners
equal to exactly the label,

00:38:06.730 --> 00:38:08.770
"v1.0."

00:38:08.770 --> 00:38:12.947
This is actually the ID, but
the label becomes the ID.

00:38:12.947 --> 00:38:14.530
And you can specify
an ID if you want,

00:38:14.530 --> 00:38:16.930
but usually you just
want the label to be

00:38:16.930 --> 00:38:19.478
the ID for easiness's sake.

00:38:19.478 --> 00:38:21.270
OK, we're going to do
the exact same thing,

00:38:21.270 --> 00:38:25.660
I'm just to copy this
to explain the 1.2 part.

00:38:25.660 --> 00:38:28.780
And it's really
just changing this.

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:31.690
We'll say, "The 1.2
build," What did it do?

00:38:31.690 --> 00:38:34.030
It added more.

00:38:34.030 --> 00:38:36.190
Well, it got better.

00:38:36.190 --> 00:38:39.520
Let's see, "Added
more missions,"

00:38:39.520 --> 00:38:41.620
basically get free DLC.

00:38:41.620 --> 00:38:52.250
"More missions and had an
updated offer agent SDK."

00:38:52.250 --> 00:38:56.310
Great, that's closed off, and
we'll say spanners = "v1.2."

00:38:56.310 --> 00:38:57.310
That's what it's called.

00:38:57.310 --> 00:38:58.810
OK, so I'm going
to run this, and we

00:38:58.810 --> 00:39:01.890
should get way more footnotes
than we've have ever seen.

00:39:01.890 --> 00:39:03.773
More footnotes, great.

00:39:03.773 --> 00:39:04.440
I see them here.

00:39:04.440 --> 00:39:07.630
One, two, and then there
should be a third here.

00:39:07.630 --> 00:39:08.130
Great.

00:39:08.130 --> 00:39:10.890
And then repeating fours.

00:39:10.890 --> 00:39:12.570
And there they all are.

00:39:12.570 --> 00:39:15.462
Great, this is so good.

00:39:15.462 --> 00:39:16.670
I'm loving this table so far.

00:39:16.670 --> 00:39:19.280
I mean it looks so great.

00:39:19.280 --> 00:39:20.030
This is fake data.

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:23.840
I wish it was real data, because
this is too good to be fake.

00:39:23.840 --> 00:39:26.460
OK, anyways, so
let's keep going.

00:39:26.460 --> 00:39:30.600
One thing I like to do is change
the widths of all the columns

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:31.100
right away.

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:34.140
I don't want it to
be left up to chance.

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:39.510
So I'll just say everything
is going to be 75 pixels wide.

00:39:39.510 --> 00:39:40.960
And let's take a look at that.

00:39:40.960 --> 00:39:44.850
This is something you do and
you just adjust it all the time.

00:39:44.850 --> 00:39:47.940
Oh no, it does not work

00:39:47.940 --> 00:39:51.120
So you've got to be careful here
not to use just everything().

00:39:51.120 --> 00:39:52.740
It's not a column name.

00:39:52.740 --> 00:39:56.010
Great, thank you.

00:39:56.010 --> 00:39:56.910
Now we should see--

00:39:56.910 --> 00:39:59.210
Yes, this looks great.

00:39:59.210 --> 00:40:02.960
Now everything is that
width, which is perfect.

00:40:02.960 --> 00:40:05.490
Here's a crazy trick.

00:40:05.490 --> 00:40:08.340
It's a trick where you
want to specify the column

00:40:08.340 --> 00:40:13.091
widths of these, but they don't
come with column names, really.

00:40:13.091 --> 00:40:15.540
You can just use
a number, like 1.

00:40:15.540 --> 00:40:18.150
And now, actually,
refer to this, not this.

00:40:18.150 --> 00:40:20.610
It's a little bit strange,
but with column widths,

00:40:20.610 --> 00:40:22.650
it assumes everything
is a column now,

00:40:22.650 --> 00:40:24.690
and we're just going
to change everything.

00:40:24.690 --> 00:40:32.020
So you do 1, and make this
a bit smaller so px(50), OK.

00:40:32.020 --> 00:40:34.510
So now that first one
should be narrowed down.

00:40:34.510 --> 00:40:36.970
As we see, it's shrunk down
a little bit, which is good.

00:40:39.890 --> 00:40:42.920
A recent version
of GT add a bunch

00:40:42.920 --> 00:40:45.200
of new functions for padding.

00:40:45.200 --> 00:40:48.110
They're all in the opt
series of functions.

00:40:48.110 --> 00:40:50.402
Once called
opt_horizontal_padding.

00:40:50.402 --> 00:40:51.860
And we're going to
do a thing where

00:40:51.860 --> 00:40:59.440
we scale it to be more padding.

00:40:59.440 --> 00:41:01.120
And I'll show where
I'm going with this.

00:41:01.120 --> 00:41:03.280
Because we have all the
values like butting up

00:41:03.280 --> 00:41:04.947
against the edge, a
little bit of space,

00:41:04.947 --> 00:41:06.590
but we want to maybe
center it a bit.

00:41:06.590 --> 00:41:07.790
I think it might look nice.

00:41:07.790 --> 00:41:09.670
So let's try it.

00:41:09.670 --> 00:41:11.850
So this will take all the
data in here, basically

00:41:11.850 --> 00:41:15.390
all the cells, it will
just bring stuff in.

00:41:15.390 --> 00:41:17.830
See, now it's more toward
the center, but still right

00:41:17.830 --> 00:41:18.330
aligned.

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:19.080
It's kind of nice.

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:21.350
And this is actually
brought in as well, so

00:41:21.350 --> 00:41:22.200
everything lines up.

00:41:22.200 --> 00:41:24.090
It's really nice.

00:41:24.090 --> 00:41:26.730
One function, it just
scales of having.

00:41:26.730 --> 00:41:29.760
And this can be between
0 and 1 as well,

00:41:29.760 --> 00:41:32.470
if you wanted to
be less padding.

00:41:32.470 --> 00:41:35.605
So that's how that works.

00:41:35.605 --> 00:41:37.230
While we're on the
padding tip, we just

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:40.420
use opt_vertical_padding
as well, because why not?

00:41:40.420 --> 00:41:42.660
And we'll try to get
this a little more dense.

00:41:42.660 --> 00:41:46.050
So we'll say the scale,
in this case, is smaller.

00:41:46.050 --> 00:41:50.960
We want less padding in
the vertical direction.

00:41:50.960 --> 00:41:53.675
So we should see a bit of a
tightening, which is great.

00:41:53.675 --> 00:41:55.550
And I'm going to make
this this a bit bigger,

00:41:55.550 --> 00:41:58.090
so you can see more of this.

00:41:58.090 --> 00:42:00.700
Good, this is really shaping up.

00:42:00.700 --> 00:42:03.120
OK, so we did the
padding thing, and now we

00:42:03.120 --> 00:42:04.495
want to do even
more style stuff.

00:42:07.050 --> 00:42:10.478
I guess we'll do
some more tab_style.

00:42:10.478 --> 00:42:12.270
Because I think what
